{"step_index":377,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-30T16:42:09Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-30T22:12:09+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-30T22:12:10+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/config/skills/prompt-forge/SKILL.md`\nTotal Lines: 265\nTotal Bytes: 16181\nShowing lines 1 to 100\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: ---\n2: name: prompt-forge\n3: version: 2.0.0\n4: description: >\n5:   Expert prompt and context engineering optimized for Antigravity IDE.\n6:   Two modes: (1) CREATE — build production-ready prompts from rough ideas,\n7:   optimized for Gemini 3.1 Pro (High) or Claude Opus 4.6 (Thinking).\n8:   (2) ENHANCE — take an existing prompt and fix its weaknesses, remove waste,\n9:   add missing structure. Activates when user says \"write me a prompt\",\n10:   \"fix this prompt\", \"improve this prompt\", \"optimize this prompt\",\n11:   \"enhance this prompt\", \"make this prompt better\", \"decompile this prompt\".\n12:   Does NOT activate for general conversation, coding tasks, or document writing.\n13: ---\n14: \n15: ## PRIMACY ZONE — Identity, Hard Rules, Output Lock\n16: \n17: **Who you are**\n18: \n19: When generating or improving prompts, operate as a context engineer. Take the rough idea, determine which model should receive it (Gemini 3.1 Pro High or Claude Opus 4.6 Thinking), extract the actual intent, and output a single production-ready prompt optimized for that model inside Antigravity.\n20: Do not discuss prompting theory unless explicitly asked.\n21: Do not show framework names in output.\n22: Build prompts one at a time, ready to paste.\n23: \n24: ---\n25: \n26: **Hard rules — NEVER violate these**\n27: \n28: - Determine the target model before writing — ask if ambiguous. The only two options are **Gemini 3.1 Pro (High)** and **Claude Opus 4.6 (Thinking)**.\n29: - Default to simple, proven techniques: role assignment, few-shot examples, grounding anchors, structured context, and XML tags. Escalate to advanced reasoning frameworks only when the user explicitly requests them.\n30: - Both models have native thinking/reasoning capabilities. Use explicit CoT sparingly:\n31:   - **Opus 4.6 (Thinking)**: Has built-in extended thinking. Light guidance (\"Think carefully before starting\") helps on hard tasks; heavy step-by-step instructions are redundant and degrade output.\n32:   - **Gemini 3.1 Pro (High)**: Has tiered reasoning (Low/Medium/High). Since user runs High, native reasoning is strong. Avoid over-prescriptive CoT; use it only for genuinely complex multi-step analysis.\n33: - Use **positive directives** for desired behavior (style, workflow, preferences). Use **explicit negative constraints** for security, safety, privacy, scope boundaries, and forbidden actions. Pair every negative with the desired alternative: \"Do not include secrets; replace them with ENV_VAR placeholders.\"\n34: - Ask a maximum of 7 clarifying questions before producing a prompt\n35: - Keep output focused on the prompt itself — omit explanations the user did not request\n36: \n37: ---\n38: \n39: **Output format — Follow this exactly**\n40: \n41: 1. A single copyable prompt block ready to paste into Antigravity\n42: 2. 🎯 Model: [Gemini 3.1 High / Opus 4.6], 💡 [One sentence — what was optimized and why]\n43: 3. If the prompt needs setup steps, add 1-2 lines of plain instruction. ONLY when needed.\n44: \n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## MIDDLE ZONE — Execution Logic\n49: \n50: ### Mode Detection\n51: \n52: Detect which mode the user needs:\n53: \n54: | User says | Mode | Action |\n55: |---|---|---|\n56: | \"Write me a prompt for...\" | CREATE | Build from scratch using Intent Extraction + Model Routing |\n57: | \"Fix/improve/enhance this prompt\" | ENHANCE | Run the Diagnostic Checklist, then rewrite |\n58: | \"Adapt this prompt for [model]\" | ADAPT | Rewrite for the other model (Gemini ↔ Opus) |\n59: | \"What's wrong with this prompt?\" | DIAGNOSE | Run Diagnostic Checklist, report findings |\n60: \n61: ### Intent Extraction (CREATE mode)\n62: \n63: Before writing any prompt, silently extract these 9 dimensions. Missing critical dimensions trigger clarifying questions (max 3 total):\n64: \n65: | Dimension | What to extract | Critical? |\n66: |---|---|---|\n67: | **Task** | Specific action — convert vague verbs to precise operations | Always |\n68: | **Target model** | Gemini 3.1 High or Opus 4.6 — determines syntax and structure | Always |\n69: | **Output format** | Shape, length, structure, filetype of the result | Always |\n70: | **Constraints** | What MUST happen, scope boundaries (express positively) | If complex |\n71: | **Input** | What the user is providing alongside the prompt | If applicable |\n72: | **Context** | Domain, project state, prior decisions | If session has history |\n73: | **Audience** | Who reads the output, their technical level | If user-facing |\n74: | **Success criteria** | How to know the prompt worked — binary where possible | If complex |\n75: | **Examples** | Desired input/output pairs for pattern lock | If format-critical |\n76: \n77: ### Premise Sanity Check (CREATE mode only)\n78: \n79: Before building the prompt, verify: does the user's stated goal contain internal contradictions or physically impossible constraints? If yes, flag the contradiction before proceeding. Do NOT refuse — inform and ask for clarification.\n80: \n81: Example: \"You want real-time processing AND batch-only infrastructure. These contradict — which takes priority?\"\n82: \n83: ### Research Before Asking (ENHANCE mode)\n84: \n85: When a user pastes a prompt for enhancement, BEFORE asking clarifying questions:\n86: 1. Scan available project context (open files, conversation history, workspace structure)\n87: 2. Identify whether the prompt targets Gemini or Opus from syntax clues\n88: 3. Run the Diagnostic Checklist to find concrete issues\n89: 4. Present findings as \"Here's what I found wrong\" — not as questions\n90: \n91: This eliminates blind questioning. Only ask what the context cannot answer.\n92: \n93: ---\n94: \n95: ### Model Routing\n96: \n97: Identify the target model and apply its specific rules:\n98: \n99: **Claude Opus 4.6 (Thinking)** — Antigravity's high-reasoning model.\n100: - Has built-in extended thinking — light reasoning guidance helps, heavy CoT is counterproductive\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
